 {"q": "Augustine compares a reader who builds up love while missing the author's meaning to a traveller who leaves the road and reaches the destination across the fields. What does he say should happen to him?", "options": ["Nothing, since he has arrived", "He should be corrected, lest he get into the habit of leaving the road and get lost", "He should be expelled", "He should read only the Psalms"], "answer": 1, "why": "The author's meaning still matters, which is one reason reading needs a community."},
 {"q": "What is the best description of sola scriptura as the Reformers held it?", "options": ["Each person reads the Bible alone and decides for themselves", "Scripture is the supreme authority, by which tradition is tested, read in the church with creeds and confessions", "Tradition has equal authority with Scripture", "Only the original languages count"], "answer": 1, "why": "Sola scriptura, not nuda scriptura."},
 {"q": "What do Jews mean by the 'oral Torah'?", "options": ["Reading the Torah aloud in synagogue", "The teaching believed given with the written Torah at Sinai and handed down, later written in the Mishnah and Talmud", "A lost book of Moses", "The Psalms"], "answer": 1, "why": "Rabbinic Judaism reads the written Torah with the oral, as Avot 1:1's chain describes."},
